  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 655 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nToday: Light Morning Showers, Drying Out this Afternoon\n\nThe surface boundary that was situated across the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley over the past day or so has shifted well south of\nthe region, taking the heavy rainfall south with it. A few\nelevated showers will percolate through the morning, mainly\nsouth of I-90, before drier air filters in from the north.\nCloudy skies likewise will start to break up by this afternoon,\nbut most locales will see more clouds than sun today. Highs\ntoday should remain seasonably cool with readings topping out in\nthe mid-60s to around 70, dictated by the exact degree of cloud\ncover we see.\n\nMonday: Potential for Showers Returns\n\nMonday into Monday night brings additional potential for \nrainfall as the upper level closed low currently over the \nnorthern Rockies shifts eastward into the Upper Midwest. \nCurrently thinking areas along and south of I-90 will be favored\nto see shower activity during this period as the current \ntrajectory of the upper low and the placement of a surface \nboundary suggests the strongest QG forcing should reside across \nthese areas. \n\nRainfall amounts should be on the lower side compared to the last \nday or so with the 20.01z NBM suggesting amounts are favored to be \nunder 1 inch with only a 10-20% chance to exceed this amount. That \nsaid, some flooding potential does exist, mainly across southeast \nMinnesota and northeast Iowa, if amounts exceed 1 inch given the \nsaturated soils, but should largely be mitigated by fairly marginal \nparameters for heavy rainfall.\n\nTuesday - Friday: Cooler and Drier\n\nCooler and more fall-like temperatures are in store for the Upper \nMississippi River Valley through much of the new week as high \npressure and lower heights become situated over the Great Lakes by \nTuesday with highs in the 60s to low 70s and lows in the 40s. A \ndrier airmass also sinks southward into the Upper Midwest during \nthis period, limiting any additional rainfall potential as we head \ninto next weekend.",
